          "heading": "Shared concept",
          "body": "The sources frame compiler optimization as a tradeoff problem whose success must be measured rather than assumed. Profile-based classic optimizations report speedup while tracking executable-size growth and using separate profiling and measurement inputs. Machine-learning compilation learns from empirical performance data but remains constrained by training quality, representativeness, and context-dependent optimization effects. Distributed quantum compilation compares remote-operation strategies under topology, depth, Bell-pair consumption, and communication-resource constraints."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Measured costs and benefits",
          "body": "Profile-based super-block optimizations are described as complements to conventional optimization, with measured speedups and controlled code growth. Machine-learning approaches can directly predict compiler choices for bounded option spaces, while examples such as thread coarsening show that a choice can improve some performance factors and harm others. Distributed quantum strategies such as entanglement swapping and data-qubit swapping are evaluated through analytical depth bounds, computational complexity, and medium-size circuit experiments."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Synthesis value",
          "body": "A synthesis page can organize compiler strategies by the costs they trade off: runtime versus code size in profile-guided optimization, training-data cost versus prediction quality in machine-learning compilation, and communication depth versus Bell-state or qubit-resource consumption in distributed quantum compilation."
